    Invalidate cached users after group changes are persisted (#8303)
    
    `JpaGroupRoleProvider` invalidated the affected users in the user cache
    *before* it persisted a group membership change. A user who was loaded
    between the invalidation and the commit was cached again with the old
    group roles and kept them until the cache entry expired.
    
    This patch moves the invalidation after the `addGroup(group)` call in
    the three methods named in #8293:
    
    - `removeMemberFromGroup`
    - `addMemberToGroup`
    - `updateGroup`
    
    As a side effect, no user is invalidated any longer if `addGroup(…)`
    throws an `UnauthorizedException`, since nothing changed in that case.
    
    I considered putting the invalidation into `addGroup(…)` itself, but
    that method does not know which members changed and is also used for
    plain group creation, so it would have to invalidate every member on
    every call. Moving the three existing calls is the smaller change and is
    what the issue suggests.
    
    This targets `r/21.x` since it is a small bug fix that changes no
    database tables, indexes, translation keys or configuration. The code is
    identical on `develop`.
    
    Fixes #8293
    
    ### How to test this patch
    
    This is a race condition, so it cannot be triggered reliably on a
    running system. The patch adds unit tests to `JpaGroupRoleProviderTest`
    instead:
    
    - For each of the three methods, a mocked `UserDirectoryService` checks
    at the moment `invalidate(…)` is called that the new membership can
    already be loaded from the database.
    - One test checks that no user is invalidated when the change is
    rejected with an `UnauthorizedException`.
    
    All four new tests fail without the change to `JpaGroupRoleProvider` and
    pass with it:
    
    ```
    ./mvnw -pl modules/userdirectory test -Dtest=JpaGroupRoleProviderTest
    ```
    
    I also ran `./mvnw -pl modules/userdirectory install`: Checkstyle
    reports 0 violations. Locally I only had JDK 27 available, on which
    `JpaUserProviderTest` fails in `setUp` with an EasyMock class-mocking
    error, with and without this patch; the other tests of the module pass.
    I did not test this on a running Opencast.
    
